WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.100
Okay.

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:04.240
I am holding a new iPhone 4S in my hand.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.410
This is the one that you can talk to and it talks back.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.260
It's a program called Siri.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:10.760
So let's try this.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.720
I'm just going to hold it up to the microphone here and push a button.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.440
Siri, where do you come from?

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.820
I, Siri, was designed by Apple in California.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.440
Where were you manufactured?

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:27.780
manufactured.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:30.880
I'm not allowed to say.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:34.680
Why?

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.360
Good question.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.620
Anything else I can do for you?

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:54.960
Can I say, I love especially, I am not allowed to say, because it implies that Siri somehow, you know, knows the answer, but she's just not allowed to tell me, which is insane because Because she's a machine.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.680
Especially because, flip over the phone, right here on the back, it's printed, assembled in China.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.920
Which, you know, we all know anyway.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.240
It's not like this is some big secret.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.820
We all know that stuff is made in China.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.000
Our phones, our computers, our clothes, our household goods.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:21.200
A couple weeks ago, I saw this one-man show where this guy did something on stage I thought was really kind of amazing.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:37.420
He took this fact that we all already know, right, this fact that our stuff is made overseas in maybe not the greatest working conditions, and he made the audience actually feel something about that fact, which, you know, is really quite a trick.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.220
You really have to know how to tell a story to be able to pull something like that off.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.780
And I bring this up because today we are excerpting that story here on the radio show.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:52.200
The guy's name is Mike Daisy, and he makes his living doing monologues on stage.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.200
He's been doing that for years.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:02:01.540
Though you're going to hear in this story that he turns himself into an amateur reporter during the course of the story, using some investigative techniques once he gets going.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.260
I think very few reporters would ever try.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.780
And finding lots of stuff I hadn't heard or seen anywhere else.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:09.660
Not like this.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.460
From WBZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:15.810
I'm Ira Glass.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.320
Today on our program, Mike Daisy takes us where Siri fears to tread.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:27.660
What you're about to hear is an excerpt of Mike Daisy's show, which he adapted for the radio and performed for a small audience.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.120
Here is Mike Daisy.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.880
My only hobby is technology.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.080
I love technology.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.960
I love everything about it.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.080
I love looking at technology.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.520
I love comparing one piece of technology with another.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.760
I love reading rumors about technology that doesn't exist yet.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.160
I love browsing technology.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.160
I love buying technology.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.720
I love opening technology.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.180
Even when it's in that bubble packaging, I love opening it.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:03:03.740
I love the smell of a new piece of technology, that sort of burnt PVC smell when you run electricity through it for the first time.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:05.400
I love that.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:19.060
And of all the kinds of technology that I love in the world, I love the technology that comes from Apple the most, because I am an Apple aficionado.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:21.900
I'm an Apple partisan.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:23.450
I'm an Apple fanboy.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:24.340
I'm a worshiper.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.620
In the cult of Mac, I have been to the House of Jobs.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.400
I have walked through the stations of his cross.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.420
I have knelt before his his throne.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:43.120
And like so many of you who may be members of this religion with me, you may know that it can be difficult at times to keep the faith.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.520
And I have strayed now and again.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.800
In the late 90s, I did sleep with a Windows system or two.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:54.060
But who didn't, really?

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:58.820
But for the most part, I have been faithful.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:08.720
But I do think it's important to understand where I sit in that hierarchy of Apple geeks for the purposes of our story.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:28.680
And so the best way I know to describe it is to say that I am at the level of geekishness where, to relax, after performances like this one, sometimes I will go back to my apartment and I will field strip my MacBook Pro into its 43 component pieces.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:35.800
I will clean them with compressed air, and I will put them back together again.

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:41.100
It soothes me.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:47.340
So the truth is, I never would have questioned this religion.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:53.960
I never would have looked deeply at this belief system because it gave me so much pleasure if it hadn't been for the pictures.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:04.440
Because one day I was relaxing on the internet, which for me means reading Macintosh news sites, which I should specify have no actual news in them.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:14.680
They're instead filled with rumors about what Apple will do next, written exclusively by people who have no goddamn idea what Apple will do next.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:18.300
But for some reason I find this soothing.

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:28.080
So I'm reading one of those news sites when this article gets posted, and it's about the fact that someone bought an iPhone and when they got it, it wasn't blank.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:32.420
It had information on it from inside the factory.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:38.320
And in fact, in the camera roll, there were pictures on it from inside the factory.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.460
And they posted these pictures into the article.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.020
And I looked at these pictures and they took my breath away.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:50.100
They're not very good pictures.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.740
They're just testing that that the camera on the phone works.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.820
They're not of anything, but I'll never forget them.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:57.940
There were four of them.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:07.580
First, there was a stack of pallets, wooden pallets stacked up, and the second one was the edge of a conveyor belt.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:11.120
And the third was totally out of focus.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.840
It could just be an enormous space.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:16.580
And the fourth was a woman.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.040
She doesn't know her picture's being taken.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.400
and she's looking off in another direction.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:23.980
She's wearing a clean suit.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:27.380
She has no expression on her face.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:45.580
And I looked at these pictures and I downloaded these pictures to my desktop and I put them in a folder on my desktop and in the weeks and months that followed, I found myself returning to them again and again, almost compulsively.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.580
I would mouse over there and I would fan them across my desktop.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.000
And I would look at them.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.680
Who are these people?

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:08.560
Because you have to understand, I have dedicated an embarrassing amount of my life to the study of these machines.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.980
I'm an amateur, but I'm a dedicated amateur.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:17.720
I understand as best I can how the hardware works and how the software rests on the hardware.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:27.780
And all that time, until I saw those pictures, it was only then I realized I had never thought ever, in a dedicated way, about how they were made.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:34.240
It's actually hard now to reconstruct what I did think.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.680
I think what I thought is they were made by robots.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:08:04.080
like I had an image in my mind that I now realize I just stole from a 60 minute story about Japanese automotive plants I just copy and pasted that I was like, plop, command V, plop it looks like that but smaller because they're laptops tops.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:23.420
I started to think how if this phone has four pictures on it taken by hand in testing, then every iPhone has four pictures on it taken in testing every iPhone in the world by hand.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:09:01.460
I started to think, and that's always a problem for any religion the moment when you begin to think shenzhen is a city without history the people who live there will tell you that because 31 years ago shenzhen was a small town a little reed huts little reed walkways between the huts the men would fish in the late afternoon.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:03.140
I hear it was lovely.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:08.320
Today, Shenzhen is a city of 14 million people.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:12.000
It is larger than New York City, depending on how you count it.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:19.400
It's the third largest city in all of China and is the place where almost all of your crap comes from.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:26.960
And the most amazing thing is, almost no one in America knows its name.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:29.590
Isn't that remarkable?

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:36.400
That there's a city where almost all of our crap comes from and no one knows its name?

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.420
I mean, we think we do know where our crap comes from.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:41.960
We're not ignorant.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:44.220
We think our crap comes comes from China.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:47.740
Right?

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:50.000
Kind of a generalized way.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:52.180
China.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.410
But it doesn't come from China.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:58.860
It comes from Shenzhen.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:00.340
It's a city.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:01.120
It's a place.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:05.960
And I am there in an elevator going down to the lobby of my hotel to meet with my translator, Kathy.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:09.220
Kathy is fascinating.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.260
She's very small.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.130
She has these sort of rounded shoulders, and she has these glasses.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:18.670
that are way too big for her face, so they keep sliding down, and she has to push them up assiduously.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:25.880
She also has this sort of unnerving habit that when she is listening to you, she leans forward indeterminately.

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:42.160
So you really get the feeling that if you were to talk to her for long enough, she would actually fall into your chest, and you'd have to pick her back up again.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:11:39.520
we go outside and get into a taxi and begin to drive to the streets of downtown Shenzhen Shenzhen looks like Blade Runner threw up on itself LEDs, neon and 15 story high video walls covered in ugly Chinese advertising it's everything they promised us the future would be we get out to the edge of the core of Shenzhen and we come to the gates because 31 years ago when Deng Xiaoping carved this area off from the rest of China with a big red pen he said this will be the special economic zone and he made a deal with the corporations he said listen use our people do whatever you want to our people just give us a modern China and the corporations took that deal and they squeezed and they squeezed and what they They got, is the Shenzhen we find today.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:44.920
And on the other side of the gates, it's the factory zone.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:49.720
And it's like going from the Eloi to the Morlocks.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:51.280
Everything changes.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.420
I've never seen anything like it.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:55.020
Everything is under construction.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:56.560
Every road has a bypass.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:58.500
Every bypass has a bypass.

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:01.760
It's bypasses all the way down.

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:05.380
We pull onto an elevated expressway.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:20.980
We begin to drive under a silver, poisoned sky because the air in Shenzhen, it's not good in Hong Kong, but when you get to Shenzhen, you can actually feel it like a booted foot pressing down on your chest.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:25.460
But it's amazing what human beings will get used to, isn't it?

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:55.100
because after you're there just a few days you hardly even notice it at all and as we're driving we're passing by arcology after arcology these immense buildings that are so large they're redefining my sense of scale moment by moment and then our taxi driver takes an exit ramp and he stops because the exit ramp stops.

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:00.960
In midair.

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:08.280
There's some rebar sticking out and an 85-foot drop to the ground.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:16.500
The only sign that the exit ramp ends is a single, solitary orange cone.

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:56.220
it's sitting there as if to say we're busy be alert we back back onto the expressway and begin to drive again and then Kathy turns to me, pushes up her glasses and says, excuse me, but I do not think this is going to work.

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:03.560
And I hasten to assure her that it will work, but I'm talking out of my ass because I don't know that it's going to work.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:07.820
In fact, I have a lot of evidence that this is not going to work.

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:45.900
In fact, all the journalists I have talked to in Hong Kong, when I tell them about my plan, you can actually see them wrestling with just how to express to me just how totally my plan is my plan is this we are in a taxi right now in the factory zone we are driving on our way to foxconn foxconn a single company makes a staggering amount of the electronics you use every day they They make electronics for Apple, Dell, Nokia, Panasonic, HP, Samsung, Sony, Lenovo, a third of all of it.

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:47.860
That's Foxconn.

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:54.580
And at this plant, they make all kinds of things, including MacBook Pros and iPhones and iPads.

00:14:54.620 --> 00:15:00.620
And so my plan is to take this taxi to the main gate, and then I'm going to get out of the taxi with my translator.

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:46.780
later and then my plan is to stand at the main gate and talk to anybody who wants to talk to me and when i tell journalists in hong kong about my plan they say that's different that's not really how we usually do things in china that's um it's really a bad idea that's really a bad idea but i don't know what else to do i have been trying to do things the right way i've been and working with a fixer for the BBC.

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:47.960
I can't get anywhere.

00:15:49.140 --> 00:16:10.160
All the doors are closed, and you reach a certain point when you realize you may need to obey your natural inclinations, and at the end of the day, I am large, I am American, and I am wearing a goddamn Hawaiian shirt.

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:19.660
And we are going to the main gates.

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:26.620
But I have to admit, when we get there, my resolve wavers.

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:32.750
Because the Foxconn plant in Shenzhen is enormous.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:40.120
The Foxconn plant in Shenzhen has 430,000 workers.

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:41.100
workers.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:45.260
That can be a difficult number to conceptualize.

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:52.400
I find it's useful to instead think about how there are more than 20 cafeterias at the plant.

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:59.920
And then you just have to understand that workers told me that these cafeterias can hold up to 10,000 people.

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:08.600
So now you just need to visualize a cafeteria that seats 10,000 people.

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:40.720
I'll wait and I get to the main gate and I get out of the taxi with my translator and the first thing I see at the gates are the guards and the guards look pissed they look really pissed and they are carrying guns.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:46.140
And I look back at the taxi, which is now pulling away.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:18:00.400
And I'm involuntarily reminded of a Google News alert that popped into my inbox a few weeks earlier about a Reuters photographer who was taking pictures not at the Foxconn plant, but near the Foxconn plant.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:05.680
And Foxconn security went out, scooped him up, and beat him before releasing him.

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:51.120
and I look up past the gates and the guards I look up at the buildings, these immense buildings they are so enormous and along the edges of each enormous building are the nets because right at the time that I am making this visit there's been an epidemic of suicides at the Foxconn plant Week after week, worker after worker has been climbing all the way up to the tops of these enormous buildings and then throwing themselves off, killing themselves in a brutal and public manner, not thinking very much about just how bad this makes Foxconn look.

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:59.900
Foxconn's response to month after month of suicides has been to put up these nets.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:09.680
It's shift change, and the workers are coming out of the plant, and I'm standing there under the hot monsoon sun in the gaze of the guards.

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:10.260
I feel ridiculous.

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:13.520
I look absurd in this landscape.

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.040
I mean, I wouldn't talk to me.

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:18.860
And Kathy surprises me.

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:20.100
Who knew?

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:22.360
She turns out to be a spitfire.

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:27.840
She runs right over to the very first worker, grabs them by the arm, drags them over to us.

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:32.660
We start talking, and in short order, we cannot keep up.

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:35.300
First there's one worker waiting.

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:36.720
Then there's two.

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:37.320
Then there's three.

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:44.940
Before long, the guards are like, and we move further and further away from the plant.

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:47.520
But the line just gets longer and longer.

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:49.080
Everyone wants to talk.

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:51.560
We start taking them three or four at a time.

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:52.800
We still can't keep up.

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:54.180
Everyone wants to talk.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:58.680
It's like they were coming to work every day thinking, you know, it'd be great.

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:29.900
it'd be so great if somebody who uses all this crap we make every day all day long it'd be so great if one of those people came and asked us what was going on because we would have stories for them and i'm just ad-hocing questions i'm asking the questions you would expect what village in china are you from how long have you been working at foxconn what do you do at the plant How do you find your job?

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:33.370
What would you change at Foxconn if you could change anything?

00:20:33.620 --> 00:20:36.360
That question always gets them.

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:45.980
They always react like a bee has flown into their faces, and then they say something to Kathy, and Kathy says, he says he never thought of that before.

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:47.620
Every time.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:48.980
Every time.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:51.720
And the stories are fascinating.

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:54.720
I talked to one young woman who works on the iPhone line.

00:20:54.720 --> 00:21:55.740
she cleans the screens of iPhones by hand in these huge racks, thousands and thousands of them every day and she shows me how she does it and I show her my iPhone and I hand her my iPhone I take a picture of her holding my iPhone and I say to her, we'll never know but you may have cleaned the screen of this iPhone when it came by you on the line, we'll never know and she, quick as a whip, she takes my phone and she rubs it against her pants and then she says there i've cleaned it a second time and i say to her you seem kind of young how old are you and she says i'm 13 and i say 13 that's young is it hard to get work at foxconn when you're she says oh no and her friends all agree they don't really check ages the outside Outside companies do have inspections, but the workers told me Foxconn always knows when there's going to be an inspection.

00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:59.260
So what they do then, they don't even check ages then.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:08.020
They just pull everyone from the affected line, and then they put the oldest workers they have on that line.

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:12.760
You'd think someone would notice this, you know?

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:18.540
I'm telling you that I do not speak Mandarin.

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:20.940
I do not speak Cantonese.

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:29.560
I have only a passing familiarity with Chinese culture, and to call what I have a passing familiarity is an insult to Chinese culture.

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:34.580
I don't know f*** all about Chinese culture.

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:49.680
But I do know that in my first two hours of my first day at that gate, I met workers who were 14 years old, Thirteen years old?

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:51.580
Twelve?

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:58.760
Do you really think Apple doesn't know?

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:17.740
In a company obsessed with the details, with the aluminum being milled just so, with the glass being fitted perfectly into the case, do you really think it's credible that they don't know?

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:25.220
or are they just doing what we're all doing?

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:30.920
Do they just see what they want to see?

00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:49.140
Emboldened by my success at Foxconn, I decide to embark on a new plan, but I'm going to need Kathy's help if it's going to work, so I meet with her in the lobby of my hotel, and I say to her, her, Kathy, now you work with a lot of American businessmen, don't you?

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:50.680
And she says, yes, I do.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:51.680
And I say, great.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:53.200
Here's what I want you to do.

00:23:53.340 --> 00:24:05.220
I want you to call all the factories you have connections with, and I want you to call them, and I want you to tell them that I am an American businessman, and that I want to buy whatever they are selling.

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:13.340
And she listens to this, and she says, but you are not a businessman.

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:18.500
And I say, that's true, I am not a businessman.

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:24.260
And she says, and you aren't going to buy their products?

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:28.400
And I say, that's true, I'm not going to buy their products.

00:24:28.940 --> 00:24:32.800
And she says, you will lie to them.

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:40.380
And I say, yes, Kathy, I'm going to lie to lots of people.

00:24:41.700 --> 00:25:17.020
and for a moment I think it isn't going to work and then you can actually see the idea leap the synaptic gap from a problem to a problem to be solved and she says you You are going to need a lot of business cards.

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:27.460
And two days later, we head out into the factory zone.

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:34.340
As we come to each factory, Kathy briefs me on what it is they make and what it is I have said I am going to buy.

00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:39.020
The factories are all different, but really they're more similar than different.

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:42.400
There's always gates and guards, and you get past those.

00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:45.120
There's always a lawn, big and green and plush.

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:46.220
No one walks on it.

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:47.240
No one uses it.

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:49.520
You go into the lobbies.

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:58.260
The lobbies are these huge, empty, Kubrickian spaces, totally empty except for a tiny little desk for the receptionist.

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:01.640
You cross the huge, empty lobby to the tiny little desk.

00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:03.960
You introduce yourselves, and then the executives.

00:26:05.100 --> 00:27:00.920
always come down in a gaggle all together they pick you up and you go up together to a conference room after the power point we head down to the factory floor industrial spaces with 20 25 30 000 workers in a single enormous space they can exert a kind of eerie fascination there's a beauty to industrialization on such a massive scale you don't have to deny it there's a wonder to seeing so much order laid out in front of you and people are walking around whispering statistics in your ear it's easy to slip into a kind of stalinist wet dream i just try to subvert that by locking on to actual faces as they take me up and down the aisles, and the first thing I notice is the silence.

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:04.180
It's so quiet.

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:15.020
At Foxconn, you're demerited if you ever speak on the line, and no factory I went to did anyone ever speak on the line, but this is deeper than that.

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:50.060
As a creature of the first world, I expect a factory making complex electronics will have the sound of machinery, but in a a place where the cost of labor is effectively zero anything that can be made by hand is made by hand no matter how complex your electronics are they are assembled by thousands and thousands of tiny little fingers working in concert and in those vast spaces the only sound is the sound of bodies in constant, unending motion.

00:27:51.940 --> 00:27:52.780
And it is constant.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:59.980
They work a Chinese hour, and a Chinese hour has 60 Chinese minutes, and a Chinese minute has 60 Chinese seconds.

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:01.740
It's not like our hour.

00:28:03.130 --> 00:28:04.120
What's our hour now?

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:07.680
46 minutes.

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:13.510
You have a bathroom break, and you have a smoke break.

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:13.880
break.

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:15.880
If you don't smoke, there's a yoga break.

00:28:18.700 --> 00:28:20.280
This doesn't look anything like that.

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:22.620
This looks like nothing we've seen in a century.

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:26.600
They work on the line, and the lines only move as fast as slow its members.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:31.080
So each person learns how to move perfectly, as quickly as possible.

00:28:31.280 --> 00:29:00.840
If they can't do it, there are people behind them watching them, and there are cameras watching both sets of people, and people watching the cameras, they lock it down they sharpen it to a fine sharp edge every hour and those hours are long the official work day in china is eight hours long that's a joke i never met anyone who'd even heard of an eight-hour shift everyone i talked to worked 12-hour shifts standard and often much much longer than that.

00:29:01.640 --> 00:29:04.880
14 hours a day, 15 hours a day.

00:29:05.500 --> 00:29:10.360
Sometimes when there's a hot new gadget coming out, you know what the f*** I'm talking about.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:17.960
Sometimes it pegs up to 16 hours a day and it just sits there for weeks and months at a time.

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:25.580
Month after month after month of straight great 16s, sometimes longer than that.

00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:32.340
While I'm in country, a worker at Foxconn dies after working a 34-hour shift.

00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:39.260
I wish I could say that's exceptional, but it's happened before.

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:43.660
I only mention it because it actually happened while I was there.

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:46.460
And I go to the dormitories.

00:29:47.000 --> 00:30:54.040
I'm a a valuable potential future customer they will show me anything i asked to see the dormitories are cement cubes 12 foot by 12 foot and in that space there are 13 beds 14 beds i count 15 beds they're stacked up like jenga puzzle pieces all the way up to the ceiling the space between them is so narrow none of us would actually fit in them they have to slide into them like coffins there are cameras in the rooms there are cameras in the hallways there are cameras everywhere and why wouldn't there be you know when we dream of a future where the regulations are washed away and the corporations are finally free to sail above us you don't have to dream about some sci-fi dystopian Blade Runner 1984 bulls**t you can go to Shenzhen tomorrow they're making your crap that way today when i leave the factories i can feel myself being rewritten from the inside out the way i see

00:30:54.440 --> 00:31:35.920
everything is starting to change i keep thinking how often do we wish more things were handmade oh we talk about that all the time don't we i wish it was like the old days i wish things had that human touch but that's not true there are more handmade things now than there have ever been in the history of the world everything is handmade I know I have been there I have seen the workers laying in parts thinner than human hair one after another after another.

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:40.120
Everything is handmade.

00:31:47.440 --> 00:31:55.000
I'm at a restaurant in the factory zone seated at a table with Kathy and this aphorism is running through my head over and over again.

00:31:55.060 --> 00:32:02.900
I can't remember who said it originally that paranoia is not paranoia when they're actually out to get you.

00:32:04.590 --> 00:32:06.520
And I go through my checklist again.

00:32:06.580 --> 00:32:12.720
I've gone through my pockets and found every slip of paper with an email address or a phone number, and I've destroyed all of these.

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:23.700
I've hidden my paper notes off of my person, and I've erased everything on my laptop, and anything I can't erase is on an encrypted partition that I hope is encrypted enough.

00:32:23.760 --> 00:33:15.600
I have done all of these things because I am in this restaurant to meet with a union because there are unions in china they're the ones that are fronts for the communist party and then there are actual unions interested in labor reform they're called secret unions because in china if you're caught being a member of or affiliating with a union like that you go to prison go to prison for many years and that's why i've had to take these precautions and getting this meeting involved climbing a ladder of associations going to meeting after meeting and each step along the way just making good my intentions just being clear that i'm a storyteller just want to hear people's stories just want to hear what they have to say and the union organizers come in and sit down and it's awkward at first and then they begin to tell me about the situation on the ground there's so

00:33:15.720 --> 00:33:26.560
much turmoil in southern china so much happening just beneath the surface they tell me about the two honda plants that have gone on strike in the the north of the province, and how they helped organize that strike.

00:33:26.680 --> 00:33:37.920
And I think about what it would mean to go on strike in a country where even being a member of a union can get you thrown in prison, what it would take to be pushed to that point.

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:42.900
And I feel provincial saying this, but it's true.

00:33:42.980 --> 00:33:46.700
I can't stop thinking about how young these people are.

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:48.900
They don't even look college-aged.

00:33:49.400 --> 00:34:29.520
they look younger than that and I say to them how do you know who's right to work with you how do you how do you find people to help you organize and this sort of breaks the narrative and for a moment they look their age and they look at each other bashfully and they say well we talk a lot we talk all the time we have lots of meetings and we meet at coffee houses and different Starbucks in Guangzhou, and we exchange papers, and sometimes there are books, and it's so clear in this moment that they are making this up as they go along.

00:34:31.980 --> 00:34:33.400
Then the workers start coming in.

00:34:33.440 --> 00:34:35.520
They come in in twos and threes and fours.

00:34:35.540 --> 00:34:36.820
They come in all day.

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:38.360
It's an eight, nine-hour day.

00:34:38.500 --> 00:34:40.360
I interview all of them.

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:41.880
Some of them are in groups.

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:44.250
There's a group that's talking about hexane.

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:47.340
N-hexane is an iPhone screen cleaner.

00:34:47.480 --> 00:34:56.700
It's great because it evaporates a little bit faster than alcohol does, which means you can run the production line even faster and try to keep up with the quotas.

00:34:56.880 --> 00:35:03.900
The problem is that N -hexane is a potent neurotoxin and all these people have been exposed.

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:06.450
Their hands shake uncontrollably.

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:09.240
Most of them can't even pick up a glass.

00:35:09.920 --> 00:35:19.080
I talk to people whose joints in their hands have disintegrated from working on the line, doing the same motion hundreds and hundreds of thousands of times.

00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:22.680
It's like carpal tunnel on a scale we can scarcely imagine.

00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:26.400
And you need to know that this is eminently avoidable.

00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:31.740
If these people were rotated monthly on their jobs, this would not happen.

00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:34.820
But that would require someone to care.

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:43.560
That would require someone at Foxconn and the other suppliers to care that would require someone at Apple and Dell and the other customers to care.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:48.800
Currently, no one in the ecosystem cares enough to even enforce that.

00:35:49.240 --> 00:36:28.740
And so when you start working at 15 or 16, by the time you are 26, 27, your hands are ruined and when they are truly ruined once they will not do anything further you know what we do with a defective part in a machine that makes machine we throw it away and the thing that unites all these people is that they are all the kind of people who would join a union in a place where joining a union can destroy your life.

00:36:29.170 --> 00:36:30.460
I talked with one woman.

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:40.320
She's very bird-like, very nervous, and she just wants to explain to me how it is that she came to be in a union because she never thought she would ever be in a union.

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:44.030
It's just that she couldn't get her company to pay her overtime.

00:36:45.280 --> 00:36:46.740
And she complained and complained.

00:36:47.120 --> 00:36:48.720
This went on for weeks and for months.

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:54.360
And Kathy says to her, kind of sharply, she says, you should have gone to the labor board.

00:36:54.660 --> 00:37:22.900
that's what they're there for you should have gone to the labor board and the woman says i did i i went to the labor board and i told them about my problem and they took down my name and my address and my company and they took my name and they put it on the blacklist and they fired me and then she shows me a copy of the blacklist a friend of hers in accounting accounting photocopied it and snuck it out to her.

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:24.020
She gives it to me.

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:25.720
I hand it to Kathy to translate.

00:37:26.420 --> 00:37:32.100
You know, in a fascist country run by thugs, you don't have to be subtle.

00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:36.680
You can say exactly what you mean.

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:41.120
The sheet is very clear that it comes from the labor board.

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:45.000
It says right across the top, the following is a list of troublemakers.

00:37:45.180 --> 00:37:47.880
If any of them are found in your Your employee dismissed them immediately.

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:56.380
Then there's just column after column after column of names, page after page after page of them.

00:37:57.160 --> 00:38:00.940
Kathy's hand trembles as she translates it.

00:38:03.860 --> 00:38:07.100
I talk to an older man with leathery skin.

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:10.060
His right hand is twisted up into a claw.

00:38:10.220 --> 00:38:13.170
It was crushed in a metal press at Foxconn.

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:17.340
He says he didn't receive any medical attention and it healed this way.

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:20.420
And then when he was too slow, they fired him.

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:23.320
Today, he works at a woodworking plant.

00:38:23.480 --> 00:38:24.780
He says he likes it better.

00:38:24.880 --> 00:38:28.920
He says the people are nicer and the hours are more reasonable.

00:38:29.260 --> 00:38:31.580
He works about 70 hours a week.

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:35.540
And I ask him what he did when he was at Foxconn.

00:38:35.920 --> 00:38:39.180
And he says he worked on the metal enclosures for the laptops.

00:38:39.420 --> 00:38:41.600
And he worked on the iPad.

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:48.460
And when he says this, I reach into my satchel and I take out my iPad.

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:59.000
And when he sees it, his eyes widen because one of the ultimate ironies of globalism, at this point, there are no iPads in China.

00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:10.840
Even though every last one that was made at factories in China, they've all been packaged up in perfectly minimalist Apple packaging and then shipped across the seas so that we can all enjoy them.

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:11.060
him.

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:18.340
He's never actually seen one on, this thing that took his hand.

00:39:20.740 --> 00:39:25.180
I turn it on, unlock the screen, and pass it to him.

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:26.980
He takes it.

00:39:27.160 --> 00:39:36.580
The icons flare into view, and he strokes the screen with his ruined hand, and the icons slide back and forth.

00:39:37.340 --> 00:39:40.080
And he says something to Kathy.

00:39:40.660 --> 00:39:48.500
And Kathy says, he says, it's a kind of magic.

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:53.500
It's a long day.

00:39:54.150 --> 00:39:58.600
At the end of it, I'm packing up everything to go, and Kathy says something to me.

00:39:58.640 --> 00:40:29.720
Out of nowhere, where she says do you think these people are mentally ill do you think it is possible they are making all this up and i look at her as though for the first time because i mean let's be clear she's my chinese worker i pay her for her time i don't think about her very much at all but But now, I really look at her.

00:40:31.720 --> 00:40:40.960
She is exactly who all these workers I've been talking to for weeks, she is exactly what they're all dreaming that their children will one day be.

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:46.320
She has a good life in the center of Shenzhen for her, for her family.

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:49.840
What does this look like to her?

00:40:52.300 --> 00:40:55.260
I say to her, what do you think?

00:40:57.280 --> 00:40:59.000
do you think they're mentally ill?

00:41:01.220 --> 00:41:04.380
And she suddenly looks very tired.

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:20.880
She takes off her glasses and she rubs the bridge of her nose and she says, no, I do not think they are mentally ill.

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:56.560
It's just that You hear stories But you do not think it is going to be so much You know It's just so much And I reach across the table And I touch her hand It's the first and last time we will ever touch I am this woman whose real name I don't even know I say to her I know exactly what you mean.

