They seemed like savvy kids, honestly.

00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:33.260
The one source that I could find that backs up Mike on this one, at least a little bit, is Apple.

00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:45.700
Apple has released a report stating in the year that Mike was in China, 2010, Apple's own auditors went into 127 facilities around the world that make its products and say they found 91 underage workers.

00:47:45.920 --> 00:47:48.140
It doesn't say which facilities the workers were at.

00:47:48.740 --> 00:48:06.220
The report states that Apple helped install systems to verify ages, educated suppliers on recruiting practices, made them return underage workers to school and made them pay pay for the kids' education, and then it stopped doing business with one supplier that had 42 underage workers and showed no commitment to addressing the problem.

00:48:12.660 --> 00:48:19.700
All this research that we did did fill in some interesting details about working conditions at Foxconn that are not in Mike Daisy's show.

00:48:20.380 --> 00:48:25.520
There's an advocacy group called SACOM, which is Students and Scholars Against Corporate Misbehavior.

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:26.660
They're based in Hong Kong.

00:48:26.660 --> 00:48:34.100
And since the suicides at Foxconn in 2010, they have put out three reports investigating conditions at the company.

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:41.120
Each report surveyed over 100 Foxconn workers and even had a researcher go undercover and take a job at the Shenzhen plant.

00:48:42.020 --> 00:48:44.200
Debbie Chan-Si Wen is a project manager at Satcom.

00:48:44.380 --> 00:48:50.100
The surveys that she did of workers depict a company where many employees have to stand all day long.

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:58.620
Because there is some research in the industry that workers who stand during work is more efficient than those who sit.

00:48:59.420 --> 00:49:04.580
And workers, they are regularly yelled at by the supervisors.

00:49:04.760 --> 00:49:09.420
And if they make some mistake, then they have to write confession letters, etc.

00:49:10.460 --> 00:49:14.300
In your documents, you call this military-style management.

00:49:15.220 --> 00:49:16.100
Why is that what you call it?

00:49:17.280 --> 00:49:21.320
I think it is the word given by some of the workers.

00:49:22.920 --> 00:49:28.760
According to Satcom's surveys and reports, the wages at Foxconn are not much more than the Chinese minimum wage.

00:49:29.720 --> 00:49:35.740
Satcom claims that it's only with tons of overtime that the money approaches what Satcom calculates as a decent living wage for a family.

00:49:35.850 --> 00:49:46.220
But probably the most surprising thing I learned about Foxconn and other Chinese electronics manufacturers from Debbie and from Ian Spalding had to do with the turnover rate.

00:49:46.900 --> 00:49:51.040
Ian Spalding says that it could be 10 to 20 percent turnover per month.

00:49:51.420 --> 00:49:54.240
He says it's a huge business problem these days in China.

00:49:54.760 --> 00:50:04.840
So you imagine the number of employees that you're hiring and that leave after one week, two week, one month on the job, and you're constantly trying to rehire people into those positions.

00:50:05.840 --> 00:50:15.210
With so many workers quitting, why doesn't that lead to companies changing conditions and raising salaries so they don't have to go through the hassle of hiring new people?

00:50:16.360 --> 00:50:17.940
Well, that's the good news.

00:50:18.200 --> 00:50:18.920
It is.

00:50:19.640 --> 00:50:24.220
Nowadays, a lot of people talk about what should companies, brands, U.S.

00:50:24.220 --> 00:50:27.400
and European brands do to make conditions better.

00:50:27.600 --> 00:50:37.560
And the reality is that actually what's proven to be more effective is this bottom-up labor market that's emerging where employees are speaking with their feet.

00:50:37.640 --> 00:50:46.640
By leaving the factory, they're forcing factories to improve wages, improve working conditions, and improving dormitories to make things more attractive for employees.

00:50:50.700 --> 00:51:00.620
When Apple turned down our invitation to come onto today's radio show, in a rather Orwellian gesture, they told us that they are 100% transparent as they refuse to come on the air.

00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:08.400
They refer to these reports that they've been issuing every year since 2007 on working conditions of the factories that make their products overseas.

00:51:09.220 --> 00:51:11.120
And these reports, I have to say, are remarkable documents.

00:51:11.380 --> 00:51:13.340
You can find these online at Apple's website.

00:51:13.920 --> 00:51:20.220
Apple, like many companies, has a code of conduct that suppliers have to commit to before they can do business with them.

00:51:20.820 --> 00:51:24.660
And each year, Apple audits many of the suppliers to make sure that they are complying with the code.

00:51:25.080 --> 00:51:29.960
If they don't, then there are corrective action plans and there's training and there's follow-up audits.

00:51:29.960 --> 00:51:35.880
It's very elaborate, and if it all fails, Apple stops buying from the supplier, or that's what they claim anyway.

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:41.380
Apple monitors pretty much all the working conditions that Mike Daisy talks about in his show.

00:51:42.040 --> 00:51:47.940
The report covering the period that Mike was in China talks about what Apple did in the wake of the suicides at Foxconn.

00:51:48.460 --> 00:51:50.110
They say they did an independent review.

00:51:50.900 --> 00:51:55.480
They asked for mental health counselors and other changes, which Apple says Foxconn has implemented.

00:51:56.180 --> 00:51:59.640
The report also has a whole section on n-hexane.

00:52:00.130 --> 00:52:05.040
Which workers, not from Foxconn, but from another plant, told Mike that they were exposed to.

00:52:05.280 --> 00:52:06.320
And they talked about it in his show.

00:52:06.880 --> 00:52:12.560
Apple says that it found 137 workers had adverse health effects after exposure to N-hexane.

00:52:13.240 --> 00:52:20.280
It says that the supplier using the stuff was told to stop using the chemical and has been audited since then to make sure it has happened.

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:22.880
Mike Daisy has read these reports.

00:52:23.720 --> 00:52:25.240
I'm glad Apple does this.

00:52:25.480 --> 00:52:27.920
It's unfortunate more companies don't do it.

00:52:27.920 --> 00:52:30.620
And I do respect them for doing it.

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:37.860
But it doesn't change the fact that the situation on the ground, even in their own reports, is not good.

00:52:37.940 --> 00:52:43.750
And then every year, the numbers are roughly the same in terms of people who are non-compliant with overtime.

00:52:43.860 --> 00:52:44.180
time.

00:52:44.360 --> 00:52:57.060
And I would say that in the 2010 report, Apple found that only 32 percent of suppliers that had audited followed its standard about working hours, though Apple doesn't name the companies that they audited in the report.

00:52:58.060 --> 00:53:00.400
And I really question the wisdom of that.

00:53:00.580 --> 00:53:13.500
I think that if they have a serious commitment to changing how things are done in the special economic zone in Shenzhen, then they would name those companies and those companies would begin to be held held responsible.

00:53:14.700 --> 00:53:19.660
As it is, Daisy says, Apple is basically saying, trust us, we're taking care of the problems.

00:53:20.360 --> 00:53:24.180
But without supplier names, nobody can independently verify any of it.

00:53:29.080 --> 00:53:36.380
Should we feel weird about the computers and phones we use, all the clothes that we wear that are made in faraway factories in Asia under harsh working conditions?

00:53:37.460 --> 00:53:40.500
Leaving Mike Daisy aside for a second, that's the question that all this raises, right?

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:46.800
And the mainstream view that you would hear from lots of economists would be, no, we shouldn't feel weird.

00:53:47.720 --> 00:53:58.600
The famously liberal Paul Krugman, winner of the Nobel Prize for Economics, New York Times columnist, has argued that in places like Indonesia, terrible factories, far, far worse than anything you've heard about here today.

00:53:58.720 --> 00:54:00.040
They raised the economy.

00:54:00.220 --> 00:54:01.220
They made everybody better off.

00:54:01.540 --> 00:54:02.850
Here's a quote from Krugman.

00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:11.400
It is the indirect and unintended results of the actions of soulless multinationals and rapacious local entrepreneurs that is not an edifying spectacle.

00:54:12.440 --> 00:54:21.680
But no matter how base the motives of those involved, the result has been to move hundreds of millions of people from abject poverty to something still awful, but nonetheless significantly better.

00:54:23.300 --> 00:54:38.240
Another person whose support for sweatshops may surprise you is one of Krugman's colleagues at the New York Times columnist, Nicholas Kristof, who was a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize for Commentary, quote, for his powerful columns that portrayed suffering among the developing world's often forgotten people.

00:54:39.340 --> 00:54:41.260
Kristof spent years reporting in Asia.

00:54:41.360 --> 00:54:43.860
He co-wrote a book about the changes going on in China.

00:54:43.860 --> 00:54:56.880
Well, I mean, if you look at Shenzhen, for example, and Guangdong, where Foxconn is, then there's no doubt that it's been a tremendous benefit, well, not only to southern China, but indeed to much of Asia.

00:54:57.780 --> 00:55:03.890
It created massive employment opportunities, especially for young women, who frankly didn't have a lot of alternatives.

00:55:03.960 --> 00:55:08.640
That tended to give women more clout within families, within the community.

00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:08.820
community.

00:55:09.360 --> 00:55:14.850
My wife's ancestral village is in southern China, not too far from Foxconn.

00:55:15.380 --> 00:55:23.500
And people in that village went from a really grim kind of lifestyle, basically in the rice paddies.

00:55:23.660 --> 00:55:31.600
And for them, and indeed for many Chinese, the grimness of factors like Foxconn was better than the grimness of the rice paddies.

00:55:32.200 --> 00:55:35.580
There's a passage of your own writing that we emailed you before you came in this morning.

00:55:35.720 --> 00:55:36.480
Did you get that?

00:55:37.030 --> 00:55:37.620
Yeah, I did.

00:55:37.840 --> 00:55:38.660
Can I ask you to read?

00:55:39.100 --> 00:55:39.800
Sure, absolutely.

00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:46.500
So this is from a New York Times Magazine article that you wrote in 2000, and the article was called Two Cheers for Sweatshops.

00:55:48.300 --> 00:55:50.920
Fourteen years ago, we moved to Asia and began reporting there.

00:55:51.480 --> 00:55:54.440
Like most Westerners, we arrived in the region outraged at sweatshops.

00:55:55.200 --> 00:55:58.060
In time, though, we came to accept the view supported by most Asians.

00:55:58.720 --> 00:56:28.840
In the years since our first conversations there, we've returned many So you're saying sweatshops are bad, but we should feel okay about it?

00:56:28.840 --> 00:56:29.340
it?

00:56:30.800 --> 00:56:38.260
Well, it's a very awkward thing to, you know, to defend sweatshops, if you will.

00:56:39.080 --> 00:56:43.880
I mean, I think it's useful to be reminded about how grim the conditions are.

00:56:44.980 --> 00:56:56.240
But again, I just think that if you try to think how you can fight poverty most effectively, and what has fought it within than China, then I think sweatshops are a key part of that answer.

00:56:56.980 --> 00:56:58.760
To that, Mike Daisy says, sure.

00:56:58.920 --> 00:56:59.680
He's heard that argument.

00:57:00.720 --> 00:57:04.920
Sweatshops are just a phase poor countries go through, a phase that all the industrialized countries already went through.

00:57:06.080 --> 00:57:16.480
But he says, even given that, don't we have some basic obligation as outsiders coming in to treat the workers the way that our country has already agreed that workers should be treated?

00:57:17.100 --> 00:57:27.880
It may be true that over the long span of time, in 100 years, people will look back and be happy about how things went, but I don't think it's as clean as that.

00:57:27.980 --> 00:57:30.920
I just want basic labor protections for people.

00:57:31.580 --> 00:57:36.740
That's entirely compatible with everything that has been flourishing in Shenzhen.

00:57:37.140 --> 00:57:43.520
It's absolutely compatible to have everything work as it has, but also people are rotated in their factories.

00:57:43.800 --> 00:57:54.900
It's entirely compatible to have things work as they are now, but there are also independent people outside of individual corporations that inspect without announcement to see that basic labor standards are covered.

00:57:55.060 --> 00:57:56.360
It's a really basic thing.

00:57:56.420 --> 00:57:58.760
It's a basic thing that we fought for in this country.

00:57:59.380 --> 00:58:03.260
It took 100 years of labor struggles to get to a place where that happened for most workers.

00:58:04.160 --> 00:58:09.340
Then we exported those jobs overseas and we didn't send the protections with them.

00:58:10.200 --> 00:58:10.820
And it's not right.

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:28.560
Mike Daisy, his one-man show about Apple is going back on stage this month in New York at the Public Theater.

00:58:28.680 --> 00:58:35.760
The full show has this entire other storyline about Steve Jobs that you'll have to buy a theater ticket if you want to hear it.

